│ patch_description        │ The current program achieves excellent performance (0.90 test accuracy, 0.45 combined     │
│                          │ score) with a very parameter-efficient design using collective parameter sharing and      │
│                          │ fixed CZ entanglement along three disjoint perfect matchings.                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To improve further while maintaining efficiency, I propose adding light parametrization   │
│                          │ to the entanglement layers themselves. This preserves the elegant matching structure      │
│                          │ while adding expressiveness: each of the three matchings will include both a fixed CZ     │
│                          │ gate (depth) and a corresponding parametrized controlled rotation gate (with shared       │
│                          │ parameters across all qubit pairs in that matching). This follows the principle of        │
│                          │ structured expressiveness without breaking the hypercube connectivity.                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The modifications:                                                                        │
│                          │ 1. After each CZ matching layer, insert a corresponding parametrized controlled rotation  │
│                          │ (CRZ, CRY, CRX respectively)                                                              │
│                          │ 2. Use shared parameters within each matching to maintain parameter efficiency            │
│                          │ 3. Total adds only 3 new unique parameters, bringing the block from 4 to 7 unique params  │
│                          │ 4. Final parameter count: 7*3*2 + 2 = 44 (up from 26 but still highly efficient)          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should improve the expressiveness of the ansatz while keeping the beneficial         │
│                          │ structure that achieved the current strong performance.                                   │
